~ start
# Dialogue that remembers what the player has already done.
#
# Flags live on Campaign, which survives walking to another map and fighting a
# battle - so a flag set here is still set an hour later. Anything can set one:
# a line below, or an Interactable with its Sets Flag field filled in.

if Campaign.flag("read_the_notice")
	Guard: So you've read the notice. Then you know why I can't let you through.
	Guard: Don't take it personally.
else
	Guard: Turn back. There's a notice by the gate if you want the long version.

# Counting, rather than a plain yes or no: the same store holds both.
do Campaign.set_flag("times_asked", Campaign.flag_value("times_asked", 0) + 1)
if Campaign.flag_value("times_asked", 0) > 2
	Guard: And asking a fourth time won't change it.

do Campaign.set_flag("spoke_to_guard")
=> END


~ notice
# What an Examine interactable might point at. Setting the flag here rather
# than on the interactable would work just as well - do whichever reads better
# for the scene.
Cyrus: "By order of the watch: the east road is closed until further notice."
do Campaign.set_flag("read_the_notice")
=> END
